---
id: org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas.post-m4
name: SceneEnginePostM4
version: 0.1.0
---

The fixes made after migration milestone M4 landed, as executable checks. They are separated from
`SPEC.mdl` — which states what the engine IS — because this file states what was WRONG and how to see
that it no longer is: every flow here began as a report against a build, and several of the first
attempts at a fix were wrong in ways only a measurement showed.

The through-line is one mistake made three times: **a quantity fixed in scene units, compared against
something measured on screen**. The grid drew levels filtered by their on-screen size while snapping
used a constant; node defaults were constant while the camera that displays them was not; text was
constant while the level it sat in was not. Each flow below pins one of those to the quantity it has
to track.

Run `moon run react-ui-canvas:e2e` for the automated subset (`src/playwright/create.spec.ts` and
`scene.spec.ts`), or the storybook at 9006 / 9009 for the human ones.

## Extensions

```mdl
ext test
  desc: An executable QA flow: actors, the requirements it covers, and steps as do/expect pairs.
  fields:
    desc?: string
    actors: ('human' | 'agent')[]
    covers: string[]
    status: 'automated' | 'partially-automated' | 'manual'
    given?: string[]
    steps: { name: string, do: string, expect: string }[]
    after?: string[]
```

## QA

```mdl
test PM4-1: A create covers the same screen area at any zoom
  desc: |
    The palette drop and the toolbar's create menu had the type's default size in scene units, so what
    they produced grew and shrank with the camera: at a nested level, where the camera arrives at a
    fraction of the parent's zoom, a new node came out a quarter of its apparent size, and drawing one
    large enough to read left its label tiny against it.

    A first fix scaled by the portal's own factor. That was unstable and is the trap worth remembering:
    `portalFrame` grows its factor until the frame contains the child's bounds, so a larger node
    enlarged the factor, which enlarged the next node. Measured in Chromium, nodes came out several
    times too big. The camera zoom is the right quantity because it does not depend on the content.
  actors: human, agent
  covers: [F-3.7]
  status: automated
  given:
    - the `SceneView` Default story (an empty canvas), at 1400x900
  steps:
    - name: Create at the fitted zoom
      do: Open the toolbar's create menu and pick the first type. Note the node's width in screen px.
      expect: a node about 250px wide
    - name: Zoom in two steps and create again
      do: Press the toolbar's zoom-in twice, then create the same way.
      expect: the new node is within a grid cell of the first one's SCREEN width, not 1.5x it
    - name: Zoom out four steps and create again
      do: Press zoom-out four times, then create the same way.
      expect: again within a cell of the first width; measured 261 / 247 / 257px at 45 / 64 / 100%
    - name: The same at depth
      do: Drill into a portal in the Nested story and create there.
      expect: the node arrives the size it would have at the root, not a quarter of it
```

```mdl
test PM4-2: A drawn box is what the pointer swept, and an empty gesture creates nothing
  desc: |
    A press that moved less than a cell used to fall back to the type's default size, so a stray click
    with a shape tool planted a node, and a small deliberate box silently became a large default one.
  actors: human, agent
  covers: [F-3.6]
  status: automated
  given:
    - the `SceneView` Default story
  steps:
    - name: Draw a wide box
      do: Press R, drag a box about 240x160 on empty canvas.
      expect: the node matches the sweep within a grid cell on each axis, and is not the default 2:1 shape
    - name: Draw a small box
      do: Press R, drag about 120x80.
      expect: the node matches that sweep; no minimum size is imposed
    - name: Click without moving
      do: Press R and click once, without moving the pointer.
      expect: NOTHING is created
```

```mdl
test PM4-3: Snapping lands on the lines that are drawn
  desc: |
    The snap unit was fixed in scene units while `Grid` keeps a level only while its cells are legible
    on screen, so the two parted company with the zoom. Far enough in, the drawn lines were four times
    finer than the snap. In a nested scene they were coarser, and a snap moved the node by less than a
    screen pixel — the "snapping doesn't work when zoomed in" report.
  actors: human, agent
  covers: [F-2.1, F-2.2, F-2.6]
  status: partially-automated
  given:
    - the `SceneView` Default story with a node on it
  steps:
    - name: Zoom out hard, then nudge
      do: Press the toolbar's zoom-out six times, then drag the node a few pixels.
      expect: it either stays put or jumps a whole visible cell; it never drifts by the few pixels dragged
    - name: Look at the lines
      do: With snapping on, drag a node slowly at any zoom.
      expect: its edges land on lines that are actually drawn — no finer mesh between them
    - name: Drill in and drag
      do: Drill into a portal and drag a node there.
      expect: snapping still lands on that level's drawn lines
    - name: Turn snapping off
      do: Press G.
      expect: the grid DISAPPEARS and the gesture is free
```

```mdl
test PM4-4: The zoom readout is against the level's own 1:1
  desc: |
    Entering a portal divides the camera by the frame's factor, so the raw number dropped fourfold on a
    drill-in that changed nothing the user could see (70% became 23%). The readout is divided by the
    same portal product. It is display only — nothing derives geometry from it, so the feedback loop
    that broke the first sizing fix cannot form here.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-1.4]
  status: manual
  given:
    - the `SceneView` Nested story
  steps:
    - name: Note the zoom, then drill in
      do: Read the toolbar's percentage, double-click a portal, and read it again.
      expect: it stays about where it was; it does not drop to a quarter
    - name: Drill back out
      do: Press Escape.
      expect: the readout returns, and depth follows the breadcrumbs
```

```mdl
test PM4-5: Links join shapes, not just ports
  desc: |
    A link could only start at a port, so joining two shapes meant aiming at a 5px dot; and with a link
    tool every node drew its ports at once, a field of dots over the diagram being drawn.
  actors: human, agent
  covers: [F-8.1, F-8.2, F-8.3]
  status: partially-automated
  given:
    - the `SceneView` Freehand story
  steps:
    - name: Ports appear on hover only
      do: Press L, then move the pointer across the canvas.
      expect: only the node under the pointer shows ports; the rest stay clean
    - name: Body to body
      do: Drag from the middle of one node to the middle of another.
      expect: a link is created between them, and no new node is created at the drop
    - name: Port still wins up close
      do: Drag from a port and drop near (not on) a port of another node.
      expect: the end binds to that port, not to the body
    - name: A node that refuses the end
      do: Drop on a node whose ports all refuse this end.
      expect: the link binds to the body rather than the drop being refused
```

```mdl
test PM4-6: A smart link is routed, not drawn
  desc: |
    First cut: a stub out of each port along its side's normal by half a major cell, one segment joining
    them, corners rounded by the same builder a spline uses. Nothing is stored, so it follows the nodes.
  actors: human, agent
  covers: [F-8.4]
  status: partially-automated
  given:
    - the `SceneView` Freehand story
  steps:
    - name: Draw one
      do: Press O, drag from one node's body to another's.
      expect: the route leaves each port straight out and turns with rounded corners
    - name: Two ports on the same side
      do: Link the east port of one node to the east port of a node below it.
      expect: the route doubles back through the middle segment rather than cutting across either node
    - name: Move a node
      do: Drag one of the linked nodes.
      expect: the route re-forms live; nothing about it was stored
```

```mdl
test PM4-7: Text reads at the level it was drawn in
  desc: |
    `style.fontSize` in the node's own scene units. The node views drop their Tailwind size class when
    it is set, which would otherwise win over the inherited value.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-3.10]
  status: manual
  given:
    - the `SceneView` Default story
  steps:
    - name: Create and read
      do: Create a node from the toolbar at any zoom and type a label into it.
      expect: the label is legible against the box, at any zoom and any depth
    - name: Override it
      do: Select the node and change `fontSize` in the properties panel.
      expect: every text part of that node takes the new size
```
